% ============================================================================
% precipitation-source-cloud — for hail, the ORIGINATING CLOUD TYPE an
% already-cited NWS Glossary sentence names, decoded as its own row
% (adj-facts-stdlib, METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `precipitation-types.adj`
% (`precip_form(precip, form)`, ONE defining physical form per precipitation
% type — hail → balls_of_ice), `precipitation-minimum-diameter.adj`
% (decoding the same hail sentence's numeric threshold), and
% `precipitation-alternate-form.adj` (decoding the same hail sentence's
% second listed descriptive term). That SAME already-quoted NWS Glossary
% sentence also names the cloud type hail falls from -- a fact none of
% those three siblings had a column for:
%
%   hail:
%     "Showery precipitation in the form of irregular pellets or balls of
%      ice more than 5 mm in diameter, falling from a cumulonimbus cloud."
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? precipitation_source_cloud(hail, $Cloud)   % cumulonimbus
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `precipitation_source_cloud(precip, cloud)`
% carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query -- the engine returns the source cloud WITH its source, on the CPU,
% with zero model calls, and abstains on any precipitation type whose own
% cited span names no originating cloud. `rain`, `snow`, `sleet`, and
% `freezing_rain` are deliberately NOT rows here: none of their own
% already-quoted spans (in `precipitation-types.adj`'s header) names a
% cloud type -- so this sibling is HONESTLY NARROWER than any of its three
% parents, grounding exactly the one precipitation type whose cited span
% names a source cloud and abstaining on any other, rather than inventing
% one. The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind a cloud and recall which
% precipitation type it produces:
%
%     ? precipitation_source_cloud($Precip, cumulonimbus)
%       % hail
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME NWS Glossary sentence
% `precipitation-types.adj`'s own header, `precipitation-minimum-diameter.adj`'s
% own header, and `precipitation-alternate-form.adj`'s own header already
% quote in full for hail -- no new WebFetch, only a different schema
% decoding the sentence's cloud-source clause as its own row. `trust
% authoritative` -- the same tier all three sibling tables already carry
% (NOAA NWS Glossary, a primary U.S. government source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table precipitation_source_cloud {
    columns precip, cloud

    row (hail, cumulonimbus)

    source "Showery precipitation in the form of irregular pellets or balls of ice more than 5 mm in diameter, falling from a cumulonimbus cloud."
    locator "https://forecast.weather.gov/glossary.php?word=HAIL"
    trust authoritative
}
